const body = `<h1>2.7c — Character Creator: Build a Party</h1>

<p>
    One more method, then you put the whole thing together and generate a party of four.
    When you are finished, <a href="../data/CharacterCheck.java" download>CharacterCheck.java</a>
    should report <code>44 passed, 0 failed</code>.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">RPG Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 5: The 5e Proficiency Table</h3>

    <p>
        This one needs no new method — you already wrote it as the last line of
        <code>generateStats()</code>. But it deserves a closer look, because it is the
        best small example of integer division in the whole course.
    </p>

    <p>
        In 5e your proficiency bonus is not something you choose; it is a function of your
        level. It starts at +2 and rises by one every four levels, topping out at +6 for a
        level 17 character.
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Level</th><td>1–4</td><td>5–8</td><td>9–12</td><td>13–16</td><td>17–20</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Bonus</th><td>+2</td><td>+3</td><td>+4</td><td>+5</td><td>+6</td></tr>
    </table>

    <p><strong>Requirement:</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <code>generateStats()</code> sets <code>proficiencyBonus</code> to
            <code>2 + (level - 1) / 4</code>
        </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Check the formula against the table. At level 4 you get
                <code>2 + 3 / 4</code> = <code>2 + 0</code>. At level 5 you get
                <code>2 + 4 / 4</code> = <code>2 + 1</code>. Why does integer division
                make this formula work?</li>
            <li>Where would the formula break if Java rounded to nearest instead of
                truncating?</li>
            <li>Look back at the tinker in 2.7a — the sheet that said
                <code>Level 9</code> and <code>Proficiency: +2</code>. Which single line of
                code would have kept those two in agreement, and why is that line not
                something you can call on its own yet?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Acceptance Test:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>Character c = new Character();
c.level = 9;
c.generateStats();
System.out.println("Level " + c.level + " -> +" + c.proficiencyBonus);  // +4</code></pre>
    <p>
        Try levels 1, 5, 9, and 17. You should see +2, +3, +4, and +6. Notice you had to
        reroll the character to change one number — <code>CharacterCheck</code> has to do
        the same thing, and it complains if <code>generateStats()</code> resets your level
        behind your back.
    </p>

    <h3>Part 6: abilityCheck(int dc, int score) — Skill Checks</h3>

    <p>
        This is the payoff for Unit 1. A skill check is a d20 roll plus the relevant
        ability modifier, against a difficulty class — and you already wrote that method.
        It is <code>Dice.skillCheck(pass, skill)</code>.
    </p>

    <p><strong>Requirements:</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>Method signature: <code>public int abilityCheck(int dc, int score)</code></li>
        <li><code>dc</code> is the difficulty to beat; <code>score</code> is the ability
            score being tested</li>
        <li>Delegates to <code>Dice.skillCheck(dc, score)</code> — do not rewrite the d20 logic</li>
        <li>Returns the same four codes as Unit 1: −1 crit fail, 0 fail, 1 success, 2 crit success</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <p>
            You are calling a method you wrote in Unit 1 rather than copying its body.
            If you later find a bug in <code>skillCheck</code>, how many places must you
            fix — and how many if <code>Character</code> kept its own copy of the d20
            logic?
        </p>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Acceptance Test:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>Character c = new Character();
c.generateStats();
System.out.println("STR check, DC 14: " + c.abilityCheck(14, c.strength));
System.out.println("INT check, DC 14: " + c.abilityCheck(14, c.intelligence));</code></pre>
    <p>
        Run each line twenty times. A high-strength character should pass a strength check
        more often than an intelligence check. If the two look the same, check that you
        are actually passing <code>score</code> through to <code>Dice.skillCheck</code>.
    </p>

    <h3>Part 7: Make It Real — Generate a Full Party</h3>

    <p>
        One character is fine. A party of four is a game. Create
        <code>CharacterTester.java</code> with a <code>main</code> method that generates
        and displays a full adventuring party.
    </p>

    <p><strong>Requirements:</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>Create 4 <code>Character</code> objects</li>
        <li>Name them (Grog the Warrior, Elara the Mage, ...)</li>
        <li>Call <code>generateStats()</code> on each</li>
        <li>Call <code>printCharacterSheet()</code> on each</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        Nothing tests <code>CharacterTester.java</code> — it is your scratch file. The
        acceptance test only ever looks at <code>Character.java</code>.
    </p>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: The Weakest Link</h3>
        <p>
            After generating all four characters, find and print the name of the character
            with the LOWEST total HP and the character with the HIGHEST strength. You will
            need to compare values across objects — for example
            <code>party[0].strength > party[1].strength</code>. The dot works on any
            object, not just on one.
        </p>
        <p>
            Before you write the loop: how do you track the best-so-far when you do not
            yet know what the best will be? What value would you start with, and why is
            starting at 0 a trap for a field that can be negative?
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Make the Party Fight</h3>
        <p>
            Give the party something to do. Use <code>abilityCheck()</code> to resolve a
            group check: pick a difficulty, have all four characters roll against it using
            their strength, and report how many succeeded. What does the return code of
            <code>2</code> mean, and how would the party react differently to a crit
            success than to an ordinary one?
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Final Socratic Checkpoint — Unit 2 Kickoff</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Trace through the entire character creator. Where is the three-step
                pattern? Which part is in <code>Character.java</code>, which is in
                <code>CharacterTester.java</code>, and which is in
                <code>CharacterCheck.java</code>?</li>
            <li>You use <code>Dice.statRoll()</code> and <code>Dice.skillCheck(dc, score)</code>
                inside your Character class. How is that different from copying the
                Math.random() formula directly into <code>Character.java</code>?</li>
            <li>Your fields are public, and the test reads them directly. Name one
                specific thing that could go wrong because of that — "it is bad
                practice" is not an answer. Then: in Unit 5 you will cause that failure
                on purpose and fix it. What do you predict the fix will be?</li>
            <li>If you wanted to add an Elf race that gets +2 DEX and −1 CON, where would
                that logic go — and which derived values would need recomputing?</li>
            <li>You have now started Unit 2. What patterns, habits, and mental models do
                you want to carry through the rest of the unit?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Create and configure multiple instances of a class</li>
            <li>Call methods on objects from a separate tester class</li>
            <li>Compare values across objects (highest / lowest)</li>
            <li>Explain the full architecture:
                <code>Dice.java</code> → <code>Character.java</code> → <code>CharacterTester.java</code></li>
            <li>Get <code>CharacterCheck.java</code> to report
                <code>44 passed, 0 failed</code></li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

